"Shen/JIT port definitions, lowered at stage 0 with the kernel. A defun here
with the name of a kernel defun replaces it: stage 0 omits the kernel's."

"An expression runs in a call, not a tail call, so that this frame stays on
the stack: backtraces stop at it. Anything but a list evaluates to itself,
which the lowerer would make a constant: load evaluates what each define
returns, (fn F)'s printer, and compiling that cost as much as the
definition."

(defun shen-jit.eval-kl (X)
  (if (cons? X)
      (let Lowered (shen-jit.lower.toplevel X ())
        (if (= (hd Lowered) defun)
            (shen-jit.install Lowered)
            (let Result ((shen-jit.install Lowered)) Result)))
      X))

"The kernel calls input+ but leaves its definition to the port (41.3)."

(defun input+ (Type Stream) (shen.input-h+ Type Stream))

"Overrides."

(defun hash (X Bound) (shen-jit.hash X Bound))

"At end of input the REPL ends the process. The kernel's REPL would report an
empty stream at every prompt, forever. Only the REPL's own read exits; a read
in user code still raises the error."

(defun shen.read-evaluate-print ()
  (do (shen-jit.mark-toplevel)
  (let Package (value shen.*package*)
    (let Lineread (shen.package-user-input Package (shen-jit.repl-read (stinput)))
      (let History (shen.update-history)
        (shen.evaluate-lineread Lineread History (value shen.*tc*)))))))

"Backtraces. The REPL's read-evaluate-print runs
inside the kernel's top-level trap-error, so it marks that handler: an error
raised while it is the innermost handler, or while none is, records the
frames on the stack, and the display prints them after the message. Errors
that the program catches record nothing."

(defun shen.toplevel-display-exception (E)
  (let Message (error-to-string E)
    (do (pr Message (stoutput))
        (pr (shen-jit.trace-text Message) (stoutput)))))

(defun shen-jit.repl-read (Stream)
  (let Byte (shen.my-read-byte Stream)
    (if (= Byte -1)
        (do (nl 1) (shen-jit.exit 0))
        (shen.read-loop Stream Byte () (lambda X (shen.return? X))))))

"The launcher exits with status 1 after reporting an error or an unknown
argument, as Shen/Scheme does; the kernel's main only reports it."

(defun shen.x.launcher.main (Argv)
  (let Result (shen.x.launcher.launch-shen Argv)
    (let Shown (shen.x.launcher.default-handle-result Result)
      (if (and (cons? Result) (element? (hd Result) (cons error (cons unknown-arguments ()))))
          (shen-jit.exit 1)
          Shown))))

"fn reads F's property list once, where the kernel's looks F up twice,
through arity and get; the results and errors are the same."

(defun fn (F)
  (let Plist (trap-error (shen.<-dict (value *property-vector*) F) (lambda E ()))
    (let Arity (trap-error (assoc arity Plist) (lambda E ()))
      (if (and (cons? Arity) (= (tl Arity) 0))
          (F)
          (let Form (trap-error (assoc shen.lambda-form Plist) (lambda E ()))
            (if (cons? Form)
                (shen-jit.lambda-form F (tl Form))
                (simple-error (cn "fn: " (shen.app F " is undefined
" shen.a)))))))))

"Lambda forms are made when fn first asks for them. Reading a definition
stores F's arity and its lambda form, which the kernel makes by evaluating
a curried lambda: one compilation per definition, about a fifth of loading
a file of definitions, for a closure that fn rarely needs. The port stores
[shen-jit.lazy-form N] instead, and fn replaces it with the real form the
first time. The kernel's shen.lambda-function calls gensym once per
argument, so the counter still moves by N, and later gensyms are the same;
the lambda made later names its parameters without gensym."

(defun shen.update-lambdatable (F N)
  (do (set shen.*gensym* (+ (value shen.*gensym*) N))
      (do (shen.set-lambda-form-entry (cons F (shen-jit.absvector3 shen-jit.lazy-form N 0)))
          N)))

(defun shen-jit.lambda-form (F Form)
  (if (shen-jit.tagged? Form shen-jit.lazy-form)
      (let Lambda (eval-kl (shen-jit.curried (cons F ()) (<-address Form 1)))
        (do (shen.set-lambda-form-entry (cons F Lambda)) Lambda))
      Form))

(defun shen-jit.curried (Call N)
  (if (= N 0)
      Call
      (let X (intern (cn "shen-jit.lambda-form" (str N)))
        (cons lambda (cons X (cons (shen-jit.curried (append Call (cons X ())) (- N 1)) ()))))))

"Native dictionaries (src/runtime/dict.c). A dictionary is an absvector
[shen.dictionary Count Buckets], with the kernel's tag at index 0 so that its
dict? and printer still apply. The buckets start at 8 whatever size is asked
for, since a large request such as the property vector's 20000 would leave
mostly empty buckets for every collection to copy; they double when the count
exceeds them, and entries change in place. A fold works on a snapshot of the entries taken
when it starts, in no particular order, so changes made by the folded
function do not affect it. A missing key builds no message when the innermost
handler ignores it (shen-jit.quiet?)."

(defun shen.dict (N)
  (if (< N 1)
      (simple-error (cn "invalid initial dict size: " (shen.app N "" shen.s)))
      (let D (absvector 3)
        (do (address-> D 0 shen.dictionary)
            (do (address-> D 1 0)
                (address-> D 2 (shen-jit.filled-vector 8 ())))))))

(defun shen.dict-count (D) (<-address D 1))

(defun shen.<-dict (D K)
  (let Entry (shen-jit.dict-find D K)
    (if (cons? Entry)
        (tl Entry)
        (simple-error (if (shen-jit.quiet?) "" (cn "value " (shen.app K " not found in dict
" shen.a)))))))

(defun shen.dict-> (D K V) (shen-jit.dict-put D K V))

(defun shen.dict-rm (D K) (shen-jit.dict-remove D K))

(defun shen.dict-fold (F D Acc) (shen-jit.fold-entries F (shen-jit.dict-entries D) Acc))

(defun shen-jit.fold-entries (F Entries Acc)
  (if (= () Entries)
      Acc
      (shen-jit.fold-entries F (tl Entries) (((F (hd (hd Entries))) (tl (hd Entries))) Acc))))

"tuple? tests a tag at index 0 in one inline primitive; the kernel's needs a
trap-error in case the absvector is empty."

(defun tuple? (X) (shen-jit.tagged? X shen.tuple))

"A Prolog variable is an immediate value holding the offset of its binding,
where the kernel allocates an absvector of its name and offset. These are all
the kernel's Prolog functions that build a variable or read its offset, so the
rest is unchanged. Compiled Prolog code calls shen.lazyderef on every argument,
and nearly every variable is one step from its value: a primitive does that
step inline, even where shen.lazyderef is itself inlined two levels deep, as in
is!."

(defun shen.make-prolog-variable (N) (shen-jit.pvar N))

"Compiled Prolog code makes every variable of a clause before its first test, each
through shen.newpv, which calls shen.ticket-number, shen.make-prolog-variable and
shen.nextticket. Here it is one function of inline primitives, with the same stores
in the same order."

(defun shen.newpv (Bindings)
  (let N (<-address Bindings 1)
    (do (address-> Bindings N shen.-null-)
        (do (address-> Bindings 1 (+ N 1))
            (shen-jit.pvar N)))))

(defun shen.pvar? (X) (shen-jit.pvar? X))

(defun shen.lazyderef (X Bindings) (shen-jit.deref X Bindings shen.-null-))

"Compiled Prolog code derefs every argument of a Shen expression, mostly variables
bound to numbers or atoms: only a pair costs a call."

(defun shen.deref (X Bindings)
  (let Value (shen.lazyderef X Bindings)
    (if (cons? Value) (shen-jit.deref-pair Value Bindings) Value)))

(defun shen-jit.deref-pair (X Bindings)
  (cons (shen.deref (hd X) Bindings) (shen.deref (tl X) Bindings)))

"(= (shen.deref X B) (shen.deref Y B)), which the Prolog compiler makes of (when (= X
Y)), compares while it dereferences, and copies neither term: the lowerer calls this
for it. It ends where they first differ, and, as shen-jit.unify= does, on a pair that
is the same object on both sides, so also on a term that holds itself, where the
copy would not end."

(defun shen-jit.deref= (X Y Bindings)
  (let A (shen.lazyderef X Bindings)
    (let B (shen.lazyderef Y Bindings)
      (if (and (cons? A) (cons? B))
          (or (shen-jit.same? A B)
              (and (shen-jit.deref= (hd A) (hd B) Bindings)
                   (shen-jit.deref= (tl A) (tl B) Bindings)))
          (= A B)))))

"Unification, as the kernel's shen.lzy= and shen.lzy=! but with no closures: it
binds what it can, keeping the variables it bound in a list, and returns that
list, or undoes them and returns false. The continuation then runs once, and a
false from it undoes them. Callers have dereferenced both terms. shen-jit.unify=!
also checks that a variable does not occur in a pair it is to be bound to. Two
lists are walked and not compared with =, which walks them too. With no occurs
check a term can hold itself, and the kernel's = ends on two such terms that are
equal: this ends on them too, as a cycle goes through a variable, and a variable
leads to one and the same object, which shen-jit.same? tells."

(defun shen-jit.undo (Trail Bindings)
  (if (cons? Trail)
      (do (shen.unwind (hd Trail) Bindings false) (shen-jit.undo (tl Trail) Bindings))
      false))

(defun shen-jit.unify= (X Y Bindings Trail)
  (cond ((and (cons? X) (cons? Y))
         (if (shen-jit.same? X Y)
             Trail
             (let A (shen.lazyderef (hd X) Bindings)
               (let D (shen.lazyderef (hd Y) Bindings)
                 (let Heads (cond ((or (cons? A) (cons? D)) (shen-jit.unify= A D Bindings Trail))
                                  ((= A D) Trail)
                                  ((shen-jit.pvar? A) (do (shen.bindv A D Bindings) (cons A Trail)))
                                  ((shen-jit.pvar? D) (do (shen.bindv D A Bindings) (cons D Trail)))
                                  (true (shen-jit.undo Trail Bindings)))
                   (if (= Heads false)
                       false
                       (shen-jit.unify= (shen.lazyderef (tl X) Bindings) (shen.lazyderef (tl Y) Bindings)
                                        Bindings Heads)))))))
        ((= X Y) Trail)
        ((shen-jit.pvar? X) (do (shen.bindv X Y Bindings) (cons X Trail)))
        ((shen-jit.pvar? Y) (do (shen.bindv Y X Bindings) (cons Y Trail)))
        (true (shen-jit.undo Trail Bindings))))

"The kernel's shen.occurs-check? on the term shen.deref builds, without building it."

(defun shen-jit.occurs-in? (Var Term Bindings)
  (if (cons? Term)
      (or (shen-jit.occurs-in? Var (shen.lazyderef (hd Term) Bindings) Bindings)
          (shen-jit.occurs-in? Var (shen.lazyderef (tl Term) Bindings) Bindings))
      (shen-jit.same? Var Term)))

(defun shen-jit.occurs? (Var Term Bindings)
  (and (cons? Term) (shen-jit.occurs-in? Var Term Bindings)))

"Heads that are not pairs are unified here, with no call, which is most of them."

(defun shen-jit.unify=! (X Y Bindings Trail)
  (cond ((and (cons? X) (cons? Y))
         (if (shen-jit.same? X Y)
             Trail
             (let A (shen.lazyderef (hd X) Bindings)
               (let D (shen.lazyderef (hd Y) Bindings)
                 (let Heads (cond ((or (cons? A) (cons? D)) (shen-jit.unify=! A D Bindings Trail))
                                  ((= A D) Trail)
                                  ((shen-jit.pvar? A) (do (shen.bindv A D Bindings) (cons A Trail)))
                                  ((shen-jit.pvar? D) (do (shen.bindv D A Bindings) (cons D Trail)))
                                  (true (shen-jit.undo Trail Bindings)))
                   (if (= Heads false)
                       false
                       (shen-jit.unify=! (shen.lazyderef (tl X) Bindings) (shen.lazyderef (tl Y) Bindings)
                                         Bindings Heads)))))))
        ((= X Y) Trail)
        ((shen-jit.pvar? X)
         (if (shen-jit.occurs? X Y Bindings)
             (shen-jit.undo Trail Bindings)
             (do (shen.bindv X Y Bindings) (cons X Trail))))
        ((shen-jit.pvar? Y)
         (if (shen-jit.occurs? Y X Bindings)
             (shen-jit.undo Trail Bindings)
             (do (shen.bindv Y X Bindings) (cons Y Trail))))
        (true (shen-jit.undo Trail Bindings))))

(defun shen-jit.unified (Trail Bindings C)
  (cond ((= Trail false) false)
        ((cons? Trail)
         (let Result (thaw C)
           (if (= Result false) (shen-jit.undo Trail Bindings) Result)))
        (true (thaw C))))

(defun shen.lzy= (X Y Bindings C)
  (shen-jit.unified (shen-jit.unify= X Y Bindings ()) Bindings C))

(defun shen.lzy=! (X Y Bindings C)
  (shen-jit.unified (shen-jit.unify=! X Y Bindings ()) Bindings C))

(defun shen.bindv (X Value Bindings)
  (address-> Bindings (shen-jit.pvar-offset X) Value))

(defun shen.unwind (X Bindings Compute)
  (do (address-> Bindings (shen-jit.pvar-offset X) shen.-null-) Compute))

"vector? tests element 0 in one inline primitive, where the kernel's needs a
trap-error in case the absvector is empty. The kernel's shen.+vector?, behind
vector patterns, builds (vector 0) on every call to compare with; this one
builds it only when element 0 is 0. @p allocates a tuple in one step. get
looks the dictionary up itself, so that a missing entry raises once, with
the kernel's messages. Like shen.<-dict, it builds no message when the
innermost handler ignores it (shen-jit.quiet?)."

(defun vector? (X) (shen-jit.vector? X))

(defun shen.+vector? (X) (and (shen-jit.vector? X) (shen-jit.nonempty-vector? X)))

(defun shen-jit.nonempty-vector? (X) (or (not (= (<-address X 0) 0)) (not (= X (vector 0)))))

"Split so that it inlines into vector patterns: a value that is not a vector
fails there, without a call. shen.+string?, behind string patterns, tests
the type before comparing with the empty string, where the kernel's
compares first; the answer is the same for every value, and a value that
is not a string fails on one inline test."

(defun shen.+string? (X) (and (string? X) (not (= X ""))))

(defun @p (X Y) (shen-jit.absvector3 shen.tuple X Y))

(defun get (X P D)
  (let Entry (trap-error (shen-jit.dict-find D X) (lambda E ()))
    (if (cons? Entry)
        (let A (assoc P (tl Entry))
          (if (empty? A)
              (simple-error (if (shen-jit.quiet?) "" (cn "attribute " (shen.app P (cn " not found for " (shen.app X "
" shen.s)) shen.s))))
              (tl A)))
        (simple-error (if (shen-jit.quiet?) "" (shen.app X (cn " has no attributes: " (shen.app P "
" shen.s)) shen.a))))))

"Sealing. Kernel functions are
sealed: user code binds calls to them once, and redefining them raises an
error. (shen-jit.seal +) seals every definition installed from then on,
until (shen-jit.seal -); SHEN_JIT_SEAL=1 and --seal start with it on.
Stage 1 registers its arity so that Shen code can call it."

(defun shen-jit.seal (X)
  (cond ((= X +) (do (shen-jit.sealing true) +))
        ((= X -) (do (shen-jit.sealing false) -))
        (true (simple-error "shen-jit.seal: expected + or -"))))

"(shen-jit.code F) is F's bytecode, from lowering its KLambda again as
define does. Kernel functions keep no
KLambda, so ps, and this, refuse them, as in Shen/Scheme. It shows the
definition factorised when it was evaluated so (shen.record-and-evaluate
below)."

(defun shen-jit.code (F)
  (shen-jit.lower.toplevel
    (if (trap-error (get F shen-jit.factorised (value *property-vector*)) (lambda E false))
        (shen.factor (ps F))
        (ps F))
    ()))

"Byte buffers and bit operations are primitives that Shen code calls by name and
may use as values, as (fn shen-jit.u8)."

(defun shen-jit.primitive (F N)
  (do (shen.initialise-arity-table (cons F (cons N ())))
      (shen.set-lambda-form-entry (cons F (shen-jit.absvector3 shen-jit.lazy-form N 0)))))

(defun shen-jit.register-primitives ()
  (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.bytes 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.bytes? 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.bytes-length 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.u8 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.u16 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.u32 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.u8! 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.u16! 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.u32! 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.string->bytes 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.bytes->string 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.copy-bytes 5)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.bit-and 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.bit-or 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.bit-xor 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.bit-not 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.shift-left 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.shift-right 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.rotate32 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.bswap32 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.popcount 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.clz 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.ctz 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pvar 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pvar? 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pvar-offset 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.deref 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.same? 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.thread 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.foreign 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer? 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer-kind 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer-address 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.bytes->pointer 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer->string 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer->bytes 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.errno 0)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.c-errno 0)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.s32 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.s64 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.f32 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.f64 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.s32! 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.s64! 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.f32! 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.f64! 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer-sized 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer! 3)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer-kill 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer-guard 2)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer-owner 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointers-dead 0)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.pointer-read-only 1)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.collect 0)
    (do (shen-jit.primitive shen-jit.write-bytes 4) shen.skip))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))

"Stage 1 calls this after the kernel and extensions are initialised: it
registers the port functions meant for Shen code. (shen-jit.exit N) ends
the process with status N; it is not named exit, since port definitions are
sealed and programs may define their own exit. (shen-jit.save-executable Path
Entry) writes a binary and ends the process. The others are for native.shen,
which would otherwise compile its calls to them as look-ups with fn.

(thread Frozen) runs Frozen in a new thread, as Shen/Scheme's does, with one
thread at a time holding a lock (thread.c). It is defined here, once sealing has
ended, since programs may define their own thread."

(defun shen-jit.initialise ()
  (do (shen-jit.register-primitives)
  (do (eval-kl (cons defun (cons thread (cons (cons x ()) (cons (cons shen-jit.thread (cons x ())) ())))))
  (do (shen.initialise-arity-table
        (cons thread (cons 1 (cons shen-jit.seal (cons 1 (cons shen-jit.backtrace (cons 0
          (cons shen-jit.exit (cons 1 (cons shen-jit.code (cons 1
            (cons shen-jit.save-executable (cons 2
              (cons shen-jit.install (cons 1 (cons shen-jit.symbol? (cons 1
                (cons shen-jit.sealed? (cons 1
                  (cons shen-jit.lower.builder (cons 3 (cons shen-jit.lower.emit (cons 2
                    (cons shen-jit.lower.bind (cons 2 (cons shen-jit.lower.reserve (cons 2
                      (cons shen-jit.lower.constant (cons 2 (cons shen-jit.lower.add-code (cons 2
                        (cons shen-jit.lower.code-form (cons 1 (cons shen-jit.lower.prim-arity (cons 1
                          (cons shen-jit.lower.fixed? (cons 1 ())))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
      (do (shen.set-lambda-form-entry (shen.lambda-entry thread))
      (do (shen.set-lambda-form-entry (shen.lambda-entry shen-jit.seal))
          (do (shen.set-lambda-form-entry (shen.lambda-entry shen-jit.exit))
              (do (shen.set-lambda-form-entry (shen.lambda-entry shen-jit.code))
                  (do (shen.set-lambda-form-entry (shen.lambda-entry shen-jit.save-executable))
                      (set shen-jit.defined ()))))))))))

"Definitions are factorised as they are evaluated, as Shen/Scheme's compiler
does: the kernel's shen.factor binds each selector once and shares the tests
of clauses that start alike, instead of recomputing (tl (tl ... X)) in every
test of every clause. Only what define produced is factorised, since
shen.factor expects its shape, and what is recorded for ps stays as define
produced it; shen.*factorise?* stays false, as in Shen/Scheme. The kernel's
shen.shendef->kldef-h, with the definition remembered:"

(defun shen.shendef->kldef-h (F Rules)
  (let Patterns (map (lambda R (fst R)) Rules)
    (let Arity (shen.arity-chk F Patterns)
      (let Checked (map (lambda R (shen.free-var-chk F R)) Rules)
        (let Kl (shen.factorise-code (shen.compile-to-kl F (shen.unprotect Rules) Arity))
          (do (set shen-jit.defined Kl) Kl))))))

"The kernel's shen.record-and-evaluate, evaluating that definition factorised:"

(defun shen.record-and-evaluate (Kl)
  (if (and (cons? Kl) (and (= defun (hd Kl)) (and (cons? (tl Kl)) (and (cons? (tl (tl Kl)))
        (and (cons? (tl (tl (tl Kl)))) (= () (tl (tl (tl (tl Kl))))))))))
      (let F (hd (tl Kl))
        (do (if (shen.sysfunc? F)
                (simple-error (shen.app F " is not a legitimate function name
" shen.a))
                shen.skip)
            (do (shen.store-arity F (length (hd (tl (tl Kl)))))
                (do (shen.record-kl F Kl)
                    (let Factor (= Kl (value shen-jit.defined))
                      (do (put F shen-jit.factorised Factor (value *property-vector*))
                          (do (eval-kl (if Factor (shen.factor Kl) Kl))
                              (shen.fn-print F))))))))
      Kl))

"absvector fills a new vector with (fail) already, so vector only sets the
length stamp; the kernel's version fills it again."

(defun vector (N) (address-> (absvector (+ N 1)) 0 N))

"<-vector is the kernel's, split in two so that each part is small enough
to inline: a failing <-address,
or either error, then happens in the caller's frame, where a quiet handler
catches it with a jump. (fail) is the symbol shen.fail!."

(defun <-vector (V N)
  (if (= N 0)
      (simple-error "cannot access 0th element of a vector
")
      (shen-jit.vector-element (<-address V N))))

"integer? is a primitive, as in Shen/Scheme: the kernel's finds a power of
two above the number and subtracts its way down, which took 21 ns for 7 and
343 ns for 123456789. A fixnum is an integer; so is a finite real without a
fraction."

(defun integer? (X) (shen-jit.integer? X))

"append copies its first list in C, in one block: the kernel's recursion
builds one pair per call on its way back. The empty list stays inline. A
list that does not end in [] raises the kernel's error."

(defun append (X Y) (if (= X ()) Y (shen-jit.append X Y)))

"The reader asks shen.misc? about every byte of a symbol that is not a
lowercase letter, digits included; the kernel's is an element? over a list
of 19 codes. The same codes as ranges: 33, 35-39, 42-43, 45-47, 60-64,
95-96 and 126. Anything but a number is not one, as with element?."

(defun shen.misc? (X)
  (and (number? X)
       (or (= X 33) (or (and (>= X 35) (<= X 39)) (or (and (>= X 42) (<= X 43))
         (or (and (>= X 45) (<= X 47)) (or (and (>= X 60) (<= X 64))
           (or (and (>= X 95) (<= X 96)) (= X 126)))))))))

"The reader's rules call shen.<-out for every output and shen.hds=? for
every one-byte rule, and the kernel's are too large to inline. The kernel's
shen.<-out tests the shape of its argument and then takes (hd (tl X)) either
way, so the test changes nothing."

(defun shen.<-out (X) (hd (tl X)))

(defun shen.hds=? (X B) (and (cons? X) (= (hd X) B)))

"Runs of bytes, which the kernel's rules read one rule call per byte, with a
parse result for each: a comment up to a return byte (9, 10 or 13, as
shen.return?), whitespace, and digits, which every token is first tried as.
Each gives what the kernel's gives."

(defun shen.<shortnatters> (Bytes) (shen.comb (shen-jit.to-return Bytes) shen.skip))

(defun shen-jit.to-return (Bytes)
  (if (cons? Bytes)
      (let B (hd Bytes)
        (if (or (= B 10) (or (= B 13) (= B 9)))
            Bytes
            (shen-jit.to-return (tl Bytes))))
      Bytes))

(defun shen.<whitespaces> (Bytes)
  (if (and (cons? Bytes) (shen.whitespace? (hd Bytes)))
      (shen.comb (shen-jit.skip-whitespace (tl Bytes)) shen.skip)
      (shen.parse-failure)))

(defun shen-jit.skip-whitespace (Bytes)
  (if (cons? Bytes)
      (let B (hd Bytes)
        (if (or (= B 32) (or (= B 10) (or (= B 13) (= B 9))))
            (shen-jit.skip-whitespace (tl Bytes))
            Bytes))
      Bytes))

(defun shen.<digits> (Bytes)
  (if (and (cons? Bytes) (shen.digit? (hd Bytes)))
      (shen-jit.digits Bytes)
      (shen.parse-failure)))

(defun shen-jit.digits (Bytes)
  (let Rest (tl Bytes)
    (if (and (cons? Rest) (shen.digit? (hd Rest)))
        (let Digits (shen-jit.digits Rest)
          (shen.comb (hd Digits) (cons (shen.byte->digit (hd Bytes)) (hd (tl Digits)))))
        (shen.comb Rest (cons (shen.byte->digit (hd Bytes)) ())))))

"A symbol's characters, joined with the same cn and n->string as the
kernel's shen.<alphanum> and shen.<alphanums>, without their rule calls."

(defun shen.<alphanums> (Bytes)
  (if (and (cons? Bytes) (or (shen.alpha? (hd Bytes)) (shen.digit? (hd Bytes))))
      (let Rest (shen.<alphanums> (tl Bytes))
        (shen.comb (hd Rest) (cn (n->string (hd Bytes)) (hd (tl Rest)))))
      (shen.comb Bytes "")))

"The reader's string-to-byte-list conversion reads one buffer backwards,
instead of copying the remaining string with tlstr at each byte."

(defun shen.str->bytes (S)
  (if (string? S)
      (let Buffer (shen-jit.string->bytes S)
        (shen-jit.buffer->list Buffer (- (shen-jit.bytes-length Buffer) 1) ()))
      (shen.f-error shen.str->bytes)))

(defun shen-jit.buffer->list (Buffer I Acc)
  (if (< I 0)
      Acc
      (shen-jit.buffer->list Buffer (- I 1) (cons (shen-jit.u8 Buffer I) Acc))))

"An atom is a string, a number or a symbol, tried in that order. Every
alternative of shen.<number> starts with a digit or one of - + . (bytes 45,
43 and 46), so the kernel's number rules, five calls deep, are tried only
on those; for the rest they fail anyway."

(defun shen.<atom> (Bytes)
  (let Str (shen.<str> Bytes)
    (if (shen.parse-failure? Str)
        (let Number (if (shen-jit.number-start? Bytes) (shen.<number> Bytes) (shen.parse-failure))
          (if (shen.parse-failure? Number)
              (let Sym (shen.<sym> Bytes)
                (if (shen.parse-failure? Sym)
                    Sym
                    (let S (shen.<-out Sym)
                      (shen.comb (shen.in-> Sym) (if (= S "<>") (cons vector (cons 0 ())) (intern S))))))
              Number))
        Str)))

(defun shen-jit.number-start? (Bytes)
  (and (cons? Bytes)
       (let B (hd Bytes) (or (shen.digit? B) (or (= B 45) (or (= B 43) (= B 46)))))))

(defun shen-jit.vector-element (X)
  (if (= X shen.fail!)
      (simple-error "vector element not found
")
      X))

"read-file, behind load and script, skips a first line that starts with #!,
so that a script can run directly. The rest
is the kernel's definition."

(defun read-file (File)
  (let Bytes (shen-jit.skip-shebang (read-file-as-bytelist File))
    (let Sexprs (trap-error (compile (lambda X (shen.<s-exprs> X)) Bytes)
                            (lambda E (shen.reader-error (value shen.*residue*))))
      (shen.process-sexprs Sexprs))))

"read-file-as-bytelist and read-file-as-string read the whole file in C, as
Shen/Scheme's do; the kernel's read one read-byte at a time, and
read-file-as-string joins the bytes with one cn each. Paths are relative to
*home-directory*, as for open."

(defun read-file-as-bytelist (File) (shen-jit.read-file File true))

(defun read-file-as-string (File) (shen-jit.read-file File false))

"pr writes a string to a byte stream at once: its bytes copied to a buffer,
then one shen-jit.write-bytes, where the kernel's takes a write-byte and a pos
under a handler for each byte, and finds the end by the error the last raises.
As there, a value that is not a string, or the empty string, writes nothing,
to any stream."

(defun pr (S Stream)
  (cond ((value *hush*) S)
        ((char-stream? Stream) (write-string S Stream))
        ((and (string? S) (not (= S "")))
         (let Bytes (shen-jit.string->bytes S)
           (do (shen-jit.write-bytes Bytes 0 (shen-jit.bytes-length Bytes) Stream) S)))
        (true S)))

"shen.record-kl, behind every definition, adds the name to shen.*userdefs*
with adjoin, which looks through the whole list, so loading n new
definitions took time growing with n squared. A name without a shen.source
property has never been recorded, so it goes on the list directly; any other
still goes through adjoin."

(defun shen.record-kl (F Kl)
  (do (set shen.*userdefs* (if (shen-jit.recorded? F)
                               (adjoin F (value shen.*userdefs*))
                               (cons F (value shen.*userdefs*))))
      (put F shen.source Kl (value *property-vector*))))

(defun shen-jit.recorded? (F)
  (let Entry (shen-jit.dict-find (value *property-vector*) F)
    (and (cons? Entry) (cons? (assoc shen.source (tl Entry))))))

(defun shen-jit.skip-shebang (Bytes)
  (if (and (cons? Bytes) (and (= (hd Bytes) 35) (and (cons? (tl Bytes)) (= (hd (tl Bytes)) 33))))
      (shen-jit.skip-line Bytes)
      Bytes))

(defun shen-jit.skip-line (Bytes)
  (cond ((= () Bytes) ())
        ((= (hd Bytes) 10) Bytes)
        (true (shen-jit.skip-line (tl Bytes)))))

"symbol? and variable? by the kernel's rules for names, in C. The kernel
tests the characters of (str X), which builds a string and scans it, and
expects str to fail on values other than atoms; shen-jit's str describes
them instead, and # < > are symbol characters, so #<procedure> would pass."

(defun symbol? (X) (> (shen-jit.symbol-kind X) 0))

(defun variable? (X) (= (shen-jit.symbol-kind X) 2))

"The reader's number overrides (docs/semantics.md, section 3). In Shen/Scheme a
zero digit adds an exact 0 to a fraction, so 8.0 reads as the integer 8;
skipping zero digits gives the same values here. Literals beyond the fixnum
range become the nearest real instead of overflowing."

(defun shen.compute-fraction-h (Digits Expt)
  (cond ((= () Digits) 0)
        ((= 0 (hd Digits)) (shen.compute-fraction-h (tl Digits) (- Expt 1)))
        (true (+ (* (shen.expt 10 Expt) (hd Digits))
                 (shen.compute-fraction-h (tl Digits) (- Expt 1))))))

(defun shen.compute-integer (Digits)
  (trap-error (shen-jit.horner Digits 0)
              (lambda E (shen-jit.decimal (shen-jit.digits-text Digits "") 0))))

(defun shen-jit.horner (Digits N)
  (if (= () Digits) N (shen-jit.horner (tl Digits) (+ (* N 10) (hd Digits)))))

(defun shen-jit.digits-text (Digits Text)
  (if (= () Digits) Text (shen-jit.digits-text (tl Digits) (cn Text (str (hd Digits))))))

(defun shen.compute-E (N Log10)
  (trap-error (* N (shen.expt 10 Log10)) (lambda E (shen-jit.decimal (str N) Log10))))
